--TEST--
every path parameter of Core\IO refuses a tainted argument, and the seven of them agree across the whole class rather than each answering for itself — a filesystem path component is an instruction because `..` and the separators direct the resolver — `rule:security/sink-predicate`, spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/sink-predicate`'s table puts a filesystem path component on the instruction
// side -- `..` and the separators direct the resolver -- so spec § 14's opening
// sentence, which calls every member of `Core\IO` a path sink, is that
// predicate applied rather than a second rule. What this file asks is that the
// class *agrees*: a member that kept an answer of its own still reads right on
// its own line and fails on this one.
tainted string $t = "note.txt";

string $content = Core\IO::read($t);
Core\IO::write($t, "x");
bool $there = Core\IO::exists($t);
uint $size = Core\IO::size($t);
Core\IO::remove($t);
Core\IO::removeDir($t);

// `within`'s *base* is a sink for the same reason. Its `$path` is the one
// laundering row in the class, and the acceptance of that row is asserted in
// tests/conformance/core/io-within-launders-a-tainted-path-and-the-sinks-take-its-answer.nvst.
string $safe = Core\IO::within($t, "note.txt");
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

